# Line endings are pinned here rather than left to each machine's
# core.autocrlf, which is what produced files showing as modified with
# byte-identical content.
#
# The working tree is LF, not CRLF. That direction matters: the editors and
# tooling used on this repo write LF, and git flags an LF file as changed
# whenever it expects CRLF on disk -- even when the blob hash is identical
# and there is nothing to commit. Storing and checking out LF means what
# tools write is already what git expects, so the churn cannot recur.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell stays CRLF. These ship as installer resources and are read by
# Windows PowerShell 5.1 and legacy editors, which are happiest with CRLF;
# they are also not touched by the tooling that caused the churn.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never apply line-ending conversion to these.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.wav binary
